      trivia: "【A Compliment Made into a Brand Name】“Macanudo” is South American slang — Argentine above all — for “wonderful” or “the best”, a term of praise for the cigar’s own quality worn as its name. It began as a size name used in Guatemala by the makers of Cuba’s Punch, and was later produced by Jamaica’s Temple Hall chiefly for the British market. General Cigar acquired the factory in 1969 and relaunched the name as an independent brand in 1971. Production moved from Cuba to Jamaica and, since 2000, to the Dominican Republic, while British-flavoured vitola names such as Hyde Park and Duke of Windsor still carry the trace of its origins in the British market."
