      trivia: "Unlike the other non-Cuban versions of famous marques, the Dunhill cigar was not born “to compete with a Cuban version”: it began when the dedicated marques a London luxury tobacconist had commissioned from Cuba as their buyer (Don Cándido, Don Alfredo, Flor del Punto) were consolidated into the single “Dunhill” trademark under the 1982 agreement with Cubatabaco. The vitola names of the Dominican Aged Range (Altamiras, Condados, Peravias, Valverdes, Samanas and the rest) avoid the Cuban-derived vocabulary of robusto and Churchill and are built as a system of their own, suggestive of Dominican place names. And what ended the brand was not the market but the convenience of capital: a few weeks after BAT announced its acquisition of Reynolds American (49.4 billion dollars), the halt of supply was decided for cigars and pipe tobacco — a business trifling in scale beside the whole."
